characteristic-neutrino-mass-scale-08a21.interpretation:
A neutrino mass scale of $m_{
u} \sim 0.1 \, \mathrm{eV}/c^2$ is a crucial benchmark in particle physics and cosmology. In seesaw mechanisms, this small mass for the light, active neutrino ($m_{
u}$) is inversely proportional to the mass of a very heavy, hypothetical right-handed neutrino ($M_R$), via a relation like $m_{
u} \approx m_D^2 / M_R$, where $m_D$ is a Dirac mass term. For $m_D$ at the electroweak scale ($\sim 100$ GeV), a 0.1 eV neutrino mass implies a grand unification scale for $M_R$ ($\sim 10^{14}$ GeV). Cosmologically, the sum of neutrino masses affects the large-scale structure of the universe, and current data from Planck and other surveys place an upper limit on this sum, $\sum m_{
u}$, which is consistent with individual masses being around or below this scale.
